new_docs = {'1990-resilio-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance-bridge-legitimacy-and-carry-forward-fragmentation-evaluation.md': "# Resilio remedy hardening attestation successor-world inheritance bridge legitimacy and carry-forward fragmentation evaluation\n\n## What current Resilio gets right\n\nCurrent official Resilio materials still deserve credit for being candid that `the old world was lost or reset`, `a new usable world exists`, and `the new world legitimately inherits the old world's standing` are not one flat truth.\nThat candor is useful.\n\nThe strongest present ingredients are:\n\n- current `If your device is stolen` docs still say the serious response path breaks the previous instance, regenerates identity, relinks devices, and reshares folders\n- current `Service files missing / Cannot identify destination folder` docs still say repair after `.sync` loss creates a new synchronization instance\n- current `How do I upgrade my Standard (1.4, or classic) folders to Advanced (2.x) folders?` docs still say there is no upgrade path and operators must remove Standard folders and re-add them as Advanced folders because of architectural differences\n- current `Sync Service Troubleshooting on Windows` docs still say switching service execution to Local System creates a new storage folder where no old added folders are visible and the operator must re-add and re-share or reconnect all folders\n- current `Running Sync as a service on Windows` docs still say one installation path migrates settings while the clean-install path requires re-sharing folders and reconnecting them to existing peers\n- current `Encrypted folders` docs still say same-database continuity matters if you want the encrypted backup peer to remain a valid recovery source, which implies that losing that lineage weakens what can be inherited\n- current `Sync Storage folder` docs still say storage contains current configuration, auxiliary settings files, and shares databases, which means successor-world carry-forward depends on which anchors actually crossed the break\n- current `Cloning Sync` docs still say cloning is unsupported and may yield multiple instances that do not transfer data to one another, which is exactly the opposite of an explicit inheritance bridge\n\n## Where the current contract still fragments\n\nThe problem is not that Resilio lacks successor-world clues.\nThe problem is that it still does not produce one first-class, case-scoped **inheritance-bridge legitimacy** object.\n\nToday an operator can often infer only weaker truths such as:\n\n- a new instance works, but it may not legitimately inherit the prior instance's standing\n- some data and even some peers reconnect, but old receipts, obligations, approvals, or waivers may or may not carry forward\n- one migration path preserves visible shares, while another path requires re-share and reconnect, yet the product still leaves inheritance meaning implicit\n- a same-database path may preserve more continuity than a regenerated-identity or new-instance path, but the product does not turn that into one explicit carry-forward budget\n- architectural conversion by remove-and-re-add may preserve user intention while still severing old-world lineage for some claims\n- a safer successor world may deserve partial inheritance of bytes or policy, while stronger authority or trust claims should remain blocked\n\nThose are useful clues.\nThey are not the same as an explicit answer to `which receipts, obligations, waivers, policy deployments, trust handles, and challenge outcomes legitimately carry forward into this successor world, which stay predecessor-bound, and which stronger continuity sentence must remain blocked?`\n\n## Why that matters for AnonSync\n\nAnonSync needs a stronger sentence than `we recovered something usable after catastrophe`.\nIt needs to support claims such as:\n\n- the old world is severed, but a named successor world may inherit bytes only\n- the successor world may inherit policy and precedent, but not old approvals or trust handles\n- a new world may inherit some obligations to notify, remediate, or revoke, even while same-world continuity remains blocked\n- carry-forward may be explicitly budgeted and ratified rather than guessed from what happened to reconnect successfully\n\nSo AnonSync should not clone Resilio's present contract at this seam.\nIt should borrow the candor about new-instance repair, identity regeneration, clean-install re-sharing, storage-world forks, architectural replacement, and same-database recovery preconditions, while replacing the fragmented operator story with one first-class family for **successor-world inheritance bridge legitimacy and carry-forward truth**.\n", '1991-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance-contract-sheet-page-predecessor-scope-carry-forward-budget-and-bridge-authority-interface-spec.md': "# Remedy-hardening-attestation successor-world inheritance contract sheet page — predecessor scope, carry-forward budget, and bridge authority\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis page is the compact contract for deciding whether a recovered or re-founded successor world may legitimately inherit standing from a predecessor world whose continuity is partial, severed, or catastrophe-bounded.\nIt exists so the product can distinguish `successor world exists`, `bytes-only carry-forward`, `policy carry-forward only`, `obligation carry-forward only`, `named receipt inherited for named slice`, `trust-handle inheritance blocked`, and `same-world continuity still blocked`.\n\n## Core fields\n\n- successor-inheritance identifier\n- source catastrophe-recovery receipt identifier\n- predecessor-world identifier\n- successor-world identifier\n- predecessor-world continuity class\n- bridge-authority source\n- rulebook governing carry-forward\n- governed slice identifier\n- predecessor artifact family under review\n- predecessor receipt or standing identifiers under review\n- predecessor-bound obligation set\n- predecessor-bound approval or trust-handle set\n- predecessor-bound policy and precedent set\n- inherited-bytes status\n- inherited-policy status\n- inherited-obligation status\n- inherited-remediation status\n- inherited-waiver or exception status\n- inherited-trust-handle status\n- inherited-approval-memory status\n- inherited-challenge or finality status\n- carry-forward budget summary\n- current successor-inheritance class\n- highest currently safe inheritance sentence\n- strongest blocked stronger sentence\n- next fact that upgrades successor-inheritance standing now\n- next fact that collapses successor-inheritance standing now\n\n## Successor-inheritance classes\n\nThe page must model at least these distinct classes:\n\n- successor world present, inheritance unreviewed\n- bytes only inherited, governance unknown\n- policy or precedent inherited, trust handles blocked\n- obligation carry-forward required, execution basis partial\n- remediation and revocation duties inherited for named slice only\n- approval memory or trust-handle inheritance blocked\n- named receipt inherited for named slice only\n- named receipt reissued rather than inherited\n- successor world ratified with bounded carry-forward budget\n- same-world continuity still blocked despite bounded inheritance\n\n## Minimum required comparisons\n\nThe page must force explicit comparison between:\n\n- same-world continuity and successor-world inheritance\n- bytes carry-forward and governance carry-forward\n- policy inheritance and trust inheritance\n- inherited obligation and inherited proof of fulfillment\n- inherited receipt and reissued replacement receipt\n- predecessor-bound authority and successor-ratified authority\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- `the new instance works` must never silently upgrade to `the old world's standing carried forward`.\n- `some peers reconnected` must never silently upgrade to `old approvals or trust handles remain valid`.\n- `we re-shared the folders` must never silently upgrade to `the old receipt was inherited`.\n- `the successor is safer` must never silently upgrade to `all predecessor obligations are discharged`.\n- `same bytes or names appeared again` must never silently upgrade to `same-world continuity restored`.\n", '1992-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance-review-page-can-this-rebuilt-or-refounded-world-legitimately-carry-the-old-worlds-standing-forward-interface-spec.md': "# Remedy-hardening-attestation successor-world inheritance review page — can this rebuilt or re-founded world legitimately carry the old world's standing forward?\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis page is the operator-facing review that answers the practical inheritance question after catastrophe recovery is already understood: given that the successor world exists, which predecessor-world truths may honestly carry forward into it, and which must remain predecessor-bound or be explicitly reissued?\n\n## Primary review prompts\n\nThe review must answer these prompts in order:\n\n1. **Which catastrophe-recovery receipt is the starting basis for this inheritance review?**\n2. **Is the successor world same-world recovery, partial-continuity successor, or explicit re-foundation?**\n3. **Which predecessor artifacts are under review for carry-forward: receipts, obligations, policies, waivers, trust handles, approvals, or precedent?**\n4. **What bridge authority or rulebook permits any inheritance here?**\n5. **Which items are inherited, which are reissued, and which remain permanently predecessor-bound?**\n6. **What is the strongest successor-inheritance sentence the product may honestly publish now?**\n\n## Review sections\n\n### 1. Predecessor-to-successor basis board\n\nShow:\n\n- source catastrophe-recovery receipt\n- predecessor world identifier\n- successor world identifier\n- strongest honest continuity sentence before inheritance review\n\n### 2. Carry-forward family board\n\nShow:\n\n- bytes and content carry-forward\n- policy and precedent carry-forward\n- obligation and remediation carry-forward\n- waiver and exception carry-forward\n- approval, trust-handle, and identity carry-forward\n- receipt inheritance versus receipt reissue\n\n### 3. Bridge-authority board\n\nShow:\n\n- governing rulebook\n- adjudicator or ratifier identity\n- whether inheritance is automatic, conditional, or prohibited\n- whether carry-forward is slice-limited or family-limited\n\n### 4. Blocker board\n\nShow:\n\n- regenerated identity blocker\nn- new synchronization-instance blocker\n- storage-world fork blocker\n- architectural-replacement blocker\n- missing predecessor anchor blocker\n- trust-handle or approval-memory blocker\n\n### 5. Inheritance sentence chooser\n\nThe review must output one and only one primary sentence class such as:\n\n- successor world exists, inheritance unreviewed\n- bytes carry forward only, governance unknown\n- policy and precedent inherited for named slice only\n- remediation and notification obligations inherited for named slice only\n- predecessor receipt cannot be inherited and must be reissued\n- trust-handle inheritance blocked, successor approval required\n- named carry-forward budget ratified for named slice only\n- same-world continuity still blocked despite bounded inheritance\n\n## Hard rules\n\nThe review must never let an operator hide:\n\n- reissue behind `inherit`\n- successor ratification behind `automatic carry-forward`\n- regenerated identity behind `same trust`\n- new-instance reconnection behind `old receipt still governs`\n- policy similarity behind `all predecessor exceptions still apply`\n", '1993-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance-proof-page-predecessor-anchor-map-carried-claims-and-inheritance-ceiling-interface-spec.md': '# Remedy-hardening-attestation successor-world inheritance proof page — predecessor anchor map, carried claims, and inheritance ceiling\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis page is the evidence-heavy proof surface for determining exactly what a successor world may legitimately inherit from its predecessor after a break, catastrophe, or forced re-foundation.\nIt proves the ceiling on any claim that tries to say the successor is not merely usable, but entitled to carry forward named predecessor-world standing.\n\n## Required evidence blocks\n\n### 1. Predecessor-world ledger\n\nPreserve:\n\n- predecessor world identifier\n- predecessor continuity class at moment of break\n- predecessor receipts and standing under review\n- predecessor obligations, waivers, approvals, and trust handles under review\n\n### 2. Successor-world ledger\n\nPreserve:\n\n- successor world identifier\n- successor creation path\n- whether successor came from same-world recovery, partial carry-forward, or explicit re-foundation\n- successor authority basis\n\n### 3. Bridge-authority ledger\n\nPreserve:\n\n- rulebook that governs carry-forward\n- ratifier or adjudicator identity\n- family-specific inheritance permissions\n- explicit prohibitions on automatic carry-forward\n\n### 4. Carry-forward ledger\n\nPreserve:\n\n- bytes inherited\n- policy or precedent inherited\n- obligations inherited\n- remediation state inherited\n- waivers or exceptions inherited\n- approvals, trust handles, and identity-derived permissions inherited or blocked\n- receipt inherited, superseded, or reissued\n\n### 5. Contradiction and downgrade ledger\n\nPreserve:\n\n- regenerated-identity exposure\n- new-instance exposure\n- storage-root fork exposure\n- architectural replacement exposure\n- missing-anchor exposure\n- contradictory inheritance witnesses\n- resulting downgrade class\n\n### 6. Claim ceiling\n\nRender:\n\n- highest honest content-carry-forward sentence\n- highest honest governance-carry-forward sentence\n- highest honest trust-carry-forward sentence\n- highest honest receipt-inheritance sentence\n- blocked stronger sentence and exact blocker\n\n## Evidence classes\n\nThe page must distinguish at least:\n\n- successor exists only\n- bytes carry-forward only\n- policy inheritance only\n- obligation inheritance only\n- receipt must be reissued\n- trust inheritance blocked\n- bounded carry-forward ratified for named slice only\n- same-world continuity still blocked\n- broader full inheritance blocked\n\n## Hard rules\n\nThe proof page must never treat:\n\n- reconnect success as proof of inherited standing\n- matching folder names as proof of shared lineage\n- migrated settings as proof every receipt still governs\n- same data bytes as proof of same approvals or trust handles\n- one inherited family as proof that all predecessor standing carried forward\n', '1994-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance-timeline-page-break-refound-map-ratify-and-reissue-events-interface-spec.md': '# Remedy-hardening-attestation successor-world inheritance timeline page — break, re-found, map, ratify, and reissue events\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis page is the ordered timeline for understanding how standing moved, failed to move, or had to be reissued across a predecessor-to-successor world boundary.\nIt exists so later operators can see whether the successor inherited anything legitimately, or only looked similar after a rebuild.\n\n## Required event families\n\nThe timeline must be able to render at least these event families:\n\n- predecessor receipt issued\n- catastrophe or world break detected\n- same-world continuity blocked\n- successor world created\n- identity regenerated\n- storage world forked\n- new synchronization instance created\n- migration or clean-install path chosen\n- predecessor artifact family nominated for inheritance review\n- bridge authority selected\n- carry-forward family ratified\n- predecessor receipt blocked from inheritance\n- replacement or successor receipt issued\n- inherited obligations accepted\n- inherited trust or approval denied\n- carry-forward budget narrowed or expanded\n- post-inheritance recertification completed\n\n## Event cards\n\nEvery event card must preserve:\n\n- event identifier\n- event class\n- timestamp\n- affected governed slice\n- predecessor class\n- resulting successor-inheritance class\n- carry-forward effect\n- blocker created or removed\n- manual step required\n- resulting stronger sentence blocked or unlocked\n\n## Fixed rendering order\n\nEvery successor-inheritance timeline must render the same sections in the same order:\n\n1. **Predecessor basis**\n2. **Break and successor-world creation**\n3. **Inheritance review and bridge-authority selection**\n4. **Reissue, ratification, and blocker outcomes**\n5. **Post-inheritance recertification and final sentence**\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- The timeline must never hide a world break behind later similarity.\n- The timeline must never hide receipt reissue behind `continuous governance`.\n- The timeline must never hide rejected trust carry-forward behind `reconnected`.\n- The timeline must never let later calm erase the predecessor-successor boundary.\n', '1995-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance-lineage-receipt-page-predecessor-scope-inherited-standing-and-blocked-stronger-continuity-sentences-interface-spec.md': '# Remedy-hardening-attestation successor-world inheritance lineage receipt page — predecessor scope, inherited standing, and blocked stronger continuity sentences\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis page is the durable receipt for the strongest honest successor-world inheritance sentence after reviewing what a rebuilt or re-founded world may legitimately carry forward from its predecessor.\nIt is the page a later operator opens when they need to know what crossed the boundary, what had to be reissued, which duties still bind, and which stronger continuity sentence remained blocked.\n\n## Required receipt fields\n\nThe receipt must preserve:\n\n- successor-inheritance identifier\n- source catastrophe-recovery receipt identifier\n- predecessor world identifier\n- successor world identifier\n- named governed slice\n- current successor-inheritance class\n- inherited-bytes status\n- inherited-policy and precedent status\n- inherited-obligation and remediation status\n- inherited-waiver or exception status\n- inherited-approval-memory status\n- inherited-trust-handle status\n- receipt inheritance versus receipt reissue status\n- bridge-authority source\n- rulebook version\n- highest honest successor-inheritance sentence\n- strongest blocked stronger sentence\n- exact blocker summary\n- next evidence needed for upgrade\n- next evidence that would force downgrade\n- issuance timestamp\n- issuer identity\n\n## Canonical sentence families\n\nThe receipt must support at least these sentence families:\n\n- the successor world exists, but inheritance has not yet been reviewed\n- bytes from the predecessor carry forward for the named slice, but governance continuity remains unproven\n- policy or precedent carries forward for the named slice, while trust handles and approval memory remain blocked\n- predecessor obligations to remediate, notify, or revoke carry forward for the named slice only\n- the predecessor receipt cannot be inherited and has been replaced by a successor receipt\n- bounded carry-forward is ratified for the named slice only\n- same-world continuity remains blocked even though a successor inheritance bridge now exists\n- broader full inheritance or universal continuity is blocked\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- The receipt must never collapse `successor works` into `predecessor standing inherited`.\n- The receipt must never collapse `reissued` into `inherited`.\n- The receipt must never collapse `same bytes` into `same trust`.\n- The receipt must never collapse `same policy intent` into `same waiver or exception state`.\n- The receipt must never collapse `bounded carry-forward` into `same-world continuity restored`.\n'}

