README_ADD = "\n\n## Revision addendum after rev0474 — remedy hardening attestation successor-world inheritance bridge legitimacy and carry-forward ceiling\n\nThis continuation archive advances the doctrine by tightening another concrete non-clone seam around **what happens after catastrophe recovery has already been reviewed honestly, but the product still has to decide whether a rebuilt, migrated, or explicitly re-founded successor world may legitimately carry any predecessor-world standing forward rather than merely looking similar after reconnection**.\nIt does eight things in one tranche:\n\n1. Continues the archive after rev0474 with a new page family centered on what happens *after* catastrophe recovery legitimacy exists but *before* the product should pretend that predecessor receipts, obligations, waivers, trust handles, or policies automatically govern the successor world.\n2. Tightens the non-clone line again: borrow Resilio's candor about stolen-device identity regeneration, `.sync`-loss repair that creates a new synchronization instance, Standard-to-Advanced remove-and-re-add replacement, clean-install versus settings-migration service setup, Local System storage forks that require re-add and re-share or reconnect, same-database preconditions for encrypted recovery, and unsupported cloning; refuse any contract where the operator still has to reconstruct `what exactly carried forward into the successor world, and what had to be reissued or stay predecessor-bound?` from scattered support articles.\n3. Adds one new **Resilio evaluation** document focused on why current remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance truth is still too fragmented to clone even though the ingredients are useful.\n4. Adds five new **interface specs** for remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance contract sheet, review, proof, timeline, and lineage receipt.\n5. Makes one hard product decision explicit: **same-world catastrophe recovery is weaker than successor-world inheritance legitimacy when continuity is no longer total.**\n6. Makes another hard product decision explicit: **a usable successor world is weaker than an explicitly ratified carry-forward budget for named predecessor artifacts.**\n7. Makes a third hard product decision explicit: **`reconnected`, `re-shared`, `migrated`, `same bytes`, and `same policy intent` are never allowed to impersonate inherited trust, inherited approvals, inherited waivers, or inherited receipt authority.**\n8. Packages the result as another continuation archive whose new tranche makes the `predecessor world / successor world / bridge authority / carry-forward family / reissue versus inherit / trust-handle blocker / bounded inheritance receipt` seam explicit in the reading order and page family.\n\nNew docs in this tranche:\n\n- `1990-resilio-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance-bridge-legitimacy-and-carry-forward-fragmentation-evaluation.md`\n- `1991-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance-contract-sheet-page-predecessor-scope-carry-forward-budget-and-bridge-authority-interface-spec.md`\n- `1992-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance-review-page-can-this-rebuilt-or-refounded-world-legitimately-carry-the-old-worlds-standing-forward-interface-spec.md`\n- `1993-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance-proof-page-predecessor-anchor-map-carried-claims-and-inheritance-ceiling-interface-spec.md`\n- `1994-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance-timeline-page-break-refound-map-ratify-and-reissue-events-interface-spec.md`\n- `1995-remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance-lineage-receipt-page-predecessor-scope-inherited-standing-and-blocked-stronger-continuity-sentences-interface-spec.md`\n"
STATUS_ADD = '\n\n## Revision addendum — successor-world inheritance bridge legitimacy and carry-forward ceiling after rev0474\n\nThe next seam after **catastrophe recovery, reconstitution legitimacy, and anchor-bounded continuity** is now explicit:\n\n- the archive can already say whether catastrophe struck, which anchors survived, whether recovery was same-world reconstitution or safe successor-world re-foundation, and which stronger same-world continuity sentence stayed blocked\n- it still needed to own the harder truth of **whether a successor world that now exists may legitimately inherit predecessor-world standing such as receipts, obligations, waivers, policy deployments, precedent, approvals, or trust handles, and under what bounded bridge authority**, because usable successor, inherited bytes only, inherited policy only, inherited obligations only, inherited trust, receipt reissued, bounded carry-forward ratified, and same-world continuity still blocked are not one consequence class\n- current Resilio docs reinforce that gap because stolen-device response explicitly breaks the prior instance and regenerates identity, `.sync`-loss repair creates a new synchronization instance, Standard-to-Advanced change requires remove-and-re-add because of architectural differences, clean-install service mode requires re-sharing and reconnecting, Local System service mode creates a new storage world with no old folders visible, and encrypted backup continuity depends on the same database surviving\n\nThis pass turns that gap into a first-class product object: **the remedy-hardening-attestation successor-world inheritance bridge legitimacy and carry-forward case**.\n\nWhat is newly true in the archive:\n\n- **catastrophe recovery legitimacy is weaker than successor-world inheritance legitimacy**\n- **usable successor only, bytes inherited, policy inherited, obligations inherited, trust inheritance blocked, predecessor receipt reissued, bounded carry-forward ratified, and same-world continuity still blocked are separate public truths**\n- **surviving or recovered bytes are weaker than inherited governance, inherited governance is weaker than inherited trust, and inherited trust is weaker than same-world continuity**\n- **reconnected peers, re-shared folders, migrated settings, same folder names, and calm after rebuild now degrade into inheritance inputs instead of impersonating carry-forward legitimacy**\n- **every serious successor-world sentence now needs one receipt that preserves predecessor world, successor world, bridge authority, inherited families, reissued families, blocked trust or approval carry-forward, highest honest inheritance sentence, and the blocked stronger continuity sentence**\n\nWhat remains intentionally true:\n\n- catastrophe recovery still matters\n- same-world reconstitution still matters\n- successor-world safety still matters\n- but none of those may substitute for one explicit answer about what the successor may inherit, what had to be reissued, and which predecessor-bound truths remain blocked forever\n'
EVAL_ADD = "\n\n## Revision addendum after rev0474 — why successor-world inheritance bridge legitimacy and carry-forward truth now sit on the non-clone side\n\nCurrent official Resilio docs are still admirably candid that `the prior world broke`, `the successor world works`, `some data or peers came back`, and `the successor legitimately inherits the predecessor's standing` are not one flat truth.\n`If your device is stolen` still says the serious response path removes shares, unlinks identity if linked, removes synced data from storage, reinstalls with settings removed, regenerates identity, relinks devices, and reshares folders, explicitly breaking connection with the stolen device.\n`Service files missing / Cannot identify destination folder` still says `.sync` loss repair creates a new synchronization instance.\n`How do I upgrade my Standard (1.4, or classic) folders to Advanced (2.x) folders?` still says there is no upgrade path because of architectural differences and that operators must remove Standard folders and re-add them as Advanced folders.\n`Running Sync as a service on Windows` still says one path migrates settings while a clean installation requires re-sharing folders and reconnecting them to already existing peers.\n`Sync Service Troubleshooting on Windows` still says running as Local System creates a new storage folder where no old added folders are visible and operators must re-add and re-share or reconnect all folders.\n`Encrypted folders` still says recovery from an encrypted backup peer depends on saved keys and on not removing the encrypted folder so the database remains the same as initially created.\n`Sync Storage folder` still says storage holds current configuration, auxiliary settings files, and shares databases.\n`Cloning Sync` still says cloning is unsupported and may create multiple instances that do not transfer data to one another.\n\nThis is strong operational candor.\nIt is also exactly why AnonSync should not clone the present contract.\nOne ordinary answer to `which predecessor-world receipts, obligations, waivers, policy deployments, approvals, and trust handles legitimately carry forward into this successor world, and which must remain blocked or be explicitly reissued?` still depends on combining reset guidance, new-instance repair, architectural replacement, migration-vs-clean-install branching, storage-world forks, same-database caveats, and anti-cloning warnings.\n\nSo this tranche freezes a stronger replacement line: **usable successor, bytes carry-forward, policy carry-forward, obligation carry-forward, trust inheritance blocked, receipt reissued, bounded carry-forward ratified, and same-world continuity still blocked become separate modeled truths.**\n\nThat is why this revision adds five more first-class pages: **Remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance contract sheet**, **successor-world-inheritance review**, **successor-world-inheritance proof**, **successor-world-inheritance timeline**, and **successor-world-inheritance lineage receipt**.\n"
SOURCES_ADD = "\n\n## rev0475 source set — successor-world inheritance bridge legitimacy and carry-forward ceiling\n\nThe most load-bearing source set for this pass was:\n\n- Resilio's current `If your device is stolen` article, which still says the serious response path removes shares, unlinks identity if linked, removes synced data from storage, reinstalls with settings removed, regenerates identity, relinks devices, and reshares folders, explicitly breaking the connection with the stolen device.\n- Resilio's current `Service files missing / Cannot identify destination folder` article, which still says `.sync` loss repair creates a new synchronization instance.\n- Resilio's current `How do I upgrade my Standard (1.4, or classic) folders to Advanced (2.x) folders?` article, which still says there is no upgrade path because of architectural differences and operators must remove Standard folders and re-add them as Advanced folders.\n- Resilio's current `Running Sync as a service on Windows` article, which still says settings migration preserves old shares while a clean installation requires re-sharing folders and reconnecting them to existing peers.\n- Resilio's current `Sync Service Troubleshooting on Windows` article, which still says running as Local System creates a new storage folder with no old added folders visible and requires re-add plus re-share or reconnect.\n- Resilio's current `Encrypted folders` article, which still says encrypted-backup recovery depends on saved keys and on not removing the encrypted folder so the database remains the same as initially created.\n- Resilio's current `Sync Storage folder` article, which still says storage keeps current configuration, auxiliary settings files, and shares databases.\n- Resilio's current `Cloning Sync` article, which still says cloning is unsupported and may create multiple instances that do not transfer data to one another.\n\nThose sources were enough to tighten the line again:\n\n- current Resilio still deserves credit for candid successor-world ingredients\n- but current Resilio still answers `what exactly may this successor world inherit from its predecessor?` too diffusely\n- AnonSync should therefore prefer explicit remedy-hardening-attestation-successor-world-inheritance sheets, reviews, proofs, timelines, and lineage receipts over overloaded reset guides, migration branches, storage-world lore, architectural replacement advice, and anti-cloning warnings\n\nPrimary sources:\n\n- If your device is stolen\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/204644049-If-your-device-is-stolen\n\n- Service files missing / Cannot identify destination folder\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/205450225-Service-files-missing-Cannot-identify-destination-folder\n\n- How do I upgrade my Standard (1.4, or classic) folders to Advanced (2.x) folders?\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/206216575-How-do-I-upgrade-my-Standard-1-4-or-classic-folders-to-Advanced-2-x-folders\n\n- Running Sync as a service on Windows\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/207701296-Running-Sync-as-a-service-on-Windows\n\n- Sync Service Troubleshooting on Windows\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/207724196-Sync-Service-Troubleshooting-on-Windows\n\n- Encrypted folders\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/207370466-Encrypted-folders\n\n- Sync Storage folder\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/206664690-Sync-Storage-folder\n\n- Cloning Sync\n  https://help.resilio.com/hc/en-us/articles/360018421940-Cloning-Sync\n"

